## Method and caveats

- {% if col.detail %}{% if col.detail.all_kept %}All {{ col.detail.candidates }} substantive posts were read in detail{% else %}{{ col.detail.selected }} of {{ col.detail.candidates }} substantive posts were chosen by code for detailed reading (every opening post, top-scored, signal-flagged, and a spread across topic clusters; at most {{ col.detail.per_person }} per person){% endif %}. Short agree or disagree replies counted as echoes rather than read: {{ col.detail.echo_replies }}.{% else %}No detail selection yet.{% endif %}

- Sentiment figures come from a separate random sample, because the posts chosen for detailed reading deliberately over-represent complaints. The sample was coded blind by an AI coder and checked against a human: {% if r.agreement_status %}{% for k, v in r.agreement_status.items() %}{{ k }} {{ v }}{% if r.alpha[k] is not none %} ({{ r.alpha[k] }}){% endif %}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}{% else %}not checked yet, so every figure is unverified{% endif %}.
- Ranges come from {{ r.bootstrap_draws }} re-draws resampling people and threads, the wider shown. They describe this collection, not a wider population.
- Forum posters are self-selected and skew towards people with strong feelings, often complaints. Treat findings as what an engaged, vocal group says, not what all users think.
- People are counted, so one prolific poster counts once.
- Ethics: posts are public but written for a community, not for research. {{ "This version leaves out quotes, thread titles and person codes." if shareable else "This internal version quotes posts; share only the default build." }}
